From 1151756449f56a8357701e1879fbce53a89ade9e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 11:08:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] i686 peephole: redundant zero-extension elimination (port
 of x86-64 redundant_ext)

The i686 accumulator emitter re-extends after every sub-int operation
because operand_to_eax cannot see the producer: video-bios.o alone
carried 43 movzbl, 15 provably redundant ("movzbl mem,%eax; ...;
movzbl %al,%eax" and post-andl re-extensions).

New eliminate_redundant_zext_i686 tracks per-family byte-ness
(bits 8..31 zero) and 16-bit-ness (bits 16..31 zero) through straight-
line code, exactly like x86-64's redundant_ext pass:
* movzbl/movzwl set the flags; same-family re-extension of a known
  byte/16-bit value is deleted; cross-family byte re-extension becomes
  a plain movl (shorter, no partial-register stall);
* Move copies propagate flags; andl $imm proves the result range from
  the immediate alone; loads/pops/setcc/unknown writers clear their
  family; implicit multi-register writers (mul/div/cltd/xchg/string
  ops) and ALL barriers (labels, calls, jumps) clear everything.
Runs before DSE with a bounded cleanup loop, since removed extensions
expose dead stores and moves.

Realmode kernel corpus: 35338 -> 34804 bytes (2.72 -> 2.67x vs GCC).
Four unit tests pin removal, label/ALU invalidation, and the andl-imm8
range proof. 835/835 unit, 50/50 correctness, regparm+cdecl ABI green,
byte/word-semantics runtime matrix green at -O0/-O2/-Os.
